/* ============================================================
   arcade/index.js — Static Arcade's game list.

   Every game here is built into StaticOS: no network, no embeds,
   loads instantly. Each entry points at a module that exports

     default { mount(el, ctx) -> { stop(), pause?(), resume?() } }

   where ctx = {
     players: 1 | 2,              chosen in the shell when a game supports 2
     best: number | null,         this device's best score so far
     onScore(n),                  live score, shown in the shell's HUD
     onEnd({ score, won, detail }), the round ended (shell shows the result)
     reduced: boolean,            prefers reduced motion
     accent: string,              the current accent colour (CSS value)
   }
   The module owns everything inside `el` (canvas or DOM), listens to
   its own keys/pointer on `el` or window while mounted, and removes
   every listener, timer and frame loop in stop().

   Metadata is real: `input` lists what the game actually reads,
   `players` what it actually supports (2 = same keyboard), `minutes`
   a typical round. `better` says which score direction wins.

   `online` marks a game that can be played against a friend on
   another device, through a room code (./room.js, over StaticOS's
   live connection): 'race' = the same puzzle side by side, each on
   your own board; 'duel' = one shared match. In an online round
   ctx also has `online`:
     { role: 'host'|'guest', seed (uint32, the same on both sides),
       peer: { name, color }, send(obj) -> bool, on(fn) -> off,
       peerScore(n) (the friend's score, for the HUD), rtt() -> ms }
   and the game ends with onEnd({ score, won: true|false|null (draw),
   versus: { me, them }, void?, detail }). `onlineRules` is the one
   line the room card shows about how a match is won.

   SHARED OPTIONS. The shell also puts these in ctx; a game that has
   no use for one ignores it, and the flags below say which games read
   which (the shell offers an option only for the games that read it):
     seed: uint32               `seeded` games: the round's course comes
                                from this seed (same seed, same round,
                                on any device); the shell picks one for
                                every round, so a run can be replayed,
                                linked ("Beat this") or shared weekly
     shared: boolean            the seed is shared with someone else (a
                                Beat-this link, the weekly challenge, a
                                tournament): use the layout that does not
                                depend on the window's shape
     ghost: null | { watch, data }  `ghosts` games: data is a replay this
                                game returned in onEnd({ replay }) from a
                                run on this seed; watch = play it back on
                                its own, else race it as a ghost
     palette: 'standard' | 'cb' (a getter) `palette` games: 'cb' swaps in
                                the colour-blind set (PALETTE_CB below),
                                and a shape or symbol on each colour
     bigText: boolean           (a getter) `bigText` games: letters and
                                words drawn larger
     speed: number 0.5–1        `speed` games: multiply the game clock by
                                it (slowed rounds never set a best)
     oneButton: boolean         `oneButton` games: playable with a single
                                button — Space, a click or a tap
     announce(text)             say something to a screen reader (the
                                shell's polite live region): board games
                                say the opponent's move with it
     keys: { [code]: code }     keys this person remapped for this game.
                                The shell already turns a pressed key
                                into the game's own key; this is for
                                prompts that name keys
     reduced                    also true while "Fewer flashes" is on
   and a game may add to onEnd: replay (any small JSON value it can
   replay deterministically from the same seed), assisted: true (an
   easier mode was on, so the round never sets a best) — or, for the
   same effect, a short sentence the result card shows instead ("A
   custom board doesn't change your best") —, vs: { key, label,
   result: 'win'|'loss'|'draw' } (a 1-player game against the
   computer: the shell keeps won–lost–drawn per level, records.js).

   `aliases` are other ids a game answers to (old links, file names).
   `modes` (each keeps its own best; ctx.mode) and `settings` (how a game
   looks or feels; ctx.settings) are explained in ./modes.js.
   ============================================================ */

